/Title (A list is suitable because it preserves daily order, supports indexing and slicing, and can grow. append\(\) is O\(1\) amortised; insert\(\) and pop\(\) near the beginning are O\(n\) because later elements shift; pop\(\) at the end is O\(1\); sort\(\) is O\(n log n\) on average.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1339 0 R
/Prev 1401 0 R
/First 1410 0 R
/Last 1411 0 R
/Count -2
>>
endobj
1403 0 obj
<<
/Title (list.insert\(index, value\) is O\(n\) in the worst case because elements after the insertion point must shift. For a year of daily records, a list is still reasonable; if late readings are frequent and date lookup is important, a dictionary keyed by date may be more suitable.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1342 0 R
/Next 1404 0 R
>>
endobj
1404 0 obj
<<
/Title (A deque\(maxlen=7\) is useful for streaming readings: append each new value, maintain a running sum, and calculate an average once seven values are present. It uses O\(7\) = O\(1\) window space. A date-keyed dictionary offers average O\(1\) lookup by date, but chronological reports require sorting dates or maintaining a separate ordered sequence. A list stores n readings in O\(n\) space; a dictionary also uses O\(n\) space with higher per-entry overhead.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1342 0 R
/Prev 1403 0 R
>>
endobj
